CAT = "cpctaPsychosocial"; SEG = "PSY"
# Domain 1 tasks 1B, 1D, 1LL to 1PP; knowledge k4 to k6, k57 to k64.
Q = [
("A worried patient says, \"I don't know if I can handle this surgery.\" Which reply is therapeutic?",
 "\"Tell me what worries you most.\"", ["\"Don't worry, you'll be fine.\"", "\"Everyone feels like that.\"", "\"You should not think that way.\""],
 "An open question invites the patient to share feelings. False reassurance and telling the patient how to feel close the conversation."),
("A patient hears much better with her left ear than her right. When you speak with her, where do you stand?",
 "On her left, facing her", ["Behind her left shoulder", "At the door, speaking loudly", "On her right, facing her"],
 "Standing on the side of her better ear, facing her in good light, helps her hear and see your lips. Shouting distorts speech."),
("A patient who speaks little English needs his meal choices explained. What is the best step?",
 "Use a qualified interpreter", ["Ask his young child to translate", "Speak louder in English", "Pick his meals for him"],
 "A trained medical interpreter gives accurate meaning and protects privacy. Using a child or guessing for the patient can lead to errors."),
("A patient's religion forbids pork. What does the technician do when the tray arrives with ham?",
 "Ask dietary for another tray", ["Tell him to eat around it", "Remove the ham and serve it", "Say it is turkey"],
 "Culturally competent care respects food rules from religion or culture. A tray with forbidden food is replaced, not just picked over."),
("A patient with dementia keeps asking for her late husband. Which response is most supportive?",
 "Invite her to talk about him", ["Remind her firmly he died", "Change the subject quickly", "Tell her he is at work"],
 "Gently inviting her to talk about him eases distress without repeating painful news or lying. Arguing with a confused patient can upset her more."),
("A technician is assigned one-to-one observation of a patient on suicide precautions. What is required?",
 "Keep him in sight at all times", ["Check on him every hour", "Leave when he is asleep", "Watch from the nurses' station"],
 "One-to-one observation means the patient is kept in direct sight continuously, including in the bathroom if policy says so, and the room is kept free of items that could cause harm."),
("Which statement from a patient is a warning sign of suicide that must be reported?",
 "\"Soon it won't matter.\"", ["\"I'm tired of hospital food.\"", "\"I miss my dog at home.\"", "\"I can't wait to go home.\""],
 "Talk of being a burden, having no future or not being around is a suicide warning sign. It is reported to the nurse right away, never kept secret."),
("A patient admitted two days ago is sweaty, shaky and anxious, and he tells you he usually drinks every day. What does this suggest?",
 "Alcohol withdrawal", ["A normal stress response", "Low blood pressure only", "An allergic reaction"],
 "Tremors, sweating, anxiety and a fast pulse a day or two after the last drink are signs of alcohol withdrawal, which can become dangerous and is reported."),
("A patient tells the technician, \"This can't be happening, the tests must be wrong.\" Which stage of grief is this?",
 "Denial", ["Anger", "Bargaining", "Acceptance"],
 "In Kübler-Ross' model, denial is refusing to believe the news. Anger, bargaining, depression and acceptance are the other four stages."),
("A patient with a new terminal diagnosis shouts at the staff, \"Why me? None of you know what you're doing!\" Which grief stage is this?",
 "Anger", ["Denial", "Bargaining", "Acceptance"],
 "Anger is a normal Kübler-Ross stage. The technician does not take it personally, stays calm and lets the nurse know the patient is struggling."),
("A hospice patient says she does not want to eat dinner tonight. What does the technician do?",
 "Respect her choice and offer later", ["Insist she finish the tray", "Feed her quickly anyway", "Remove her water pitcher"],
 "In end-of-life care, appetite falls and forcing food adds discomfort. Comfort and the patient's wishes guide care; the change is mentioned to the nurse."),
("A dying patient's mouth is dry because she is breathing through it. What comfort care helps?",
 "Mouth care and lip balm", ["Large glasses of water", "Leaving the mouth alone", "Hard candy to suck on"],
 "Frequent gentle mouth care and lip balm keep the mouth comfortable. Forcing fluids or hard candy can cause choking."),
("After a patient dies, how is the body positioned during postmortem care?",
 "Supine with a head pillow", ["Prone with arms over the head", "On the left side, knees bent", "Sitting upright in a chair"],
 "The body is placed flat on the back with a pillow under the head to keep blood from pooling and discoloring the face, and the eyes are gently closed."),
("A family member is crying loudly and angry at the staff after a death. What does the technician do?",
 "Stay calm and listen", ["Ask her to leave the room now", "Argue with her about care", "Tell her to stop crying"],
 "Anger is a normal part of grief. Listening calmly and offering privacy supports the family; the nurse is told if the family needs more help."),
("Under the Five Rights of Delegation, which is one of the rights?",
 "Right circumstance", ["Right dose", "Right route", "Right documentation"],
 "The five rights of delegation are right task, right circumstance, right person, right direction and communication, and right supervision. Dose and route belong to the rights of medication administration."),
("A nurse asks the technician to give a patient's insulin injection. The technician is not trained or allowed to give medications. Which delegation right is missing?",
 "Right task", ["Right circumstance", "Right supervision", "Right documentation"],
 "Giving insulin is not a task that can be delegated to a technician, so the right task is missing. The technician explains this and the nurse gives the medication."),
]
